@startuml PassTheKeysDpopExtDetailed
title Defended with the `dpop` WebAuthn extension - the authenticator mints and names a per-session key

skinparam shadowing false
skinparam defaultFontName sans-serif
skinparam sequenceMessageAlign center
skinparam ParticipantPadding 14
skinparam BoxPadding 12

actor "Victim" as V
box "The victim's device" #E7F6EC
  participant "Authenticator\n(passkey credential + the dpop extension)" as AU
  participant "Victim's tab (/login)\n+ PasskeyProvider" as VP
end box
participant "Relying party\nverifies the assertion, binds the session" as RP
box "Attacker's site (separate origin)" #FDECEC
  participant "Attacker's tab" as AP
  participant "Back-end\ndead-drop + proxy to the RP" as AD
end box
actor "Attacker" as A

note across
  The binding key is generated INSIDE the authenticator, fresh for each login, and named by it.
  The page never holds, derives, or chooses it. SK_pub travels inside the credential-signed
  authenticatorData, so a page running attacker code can neither read it out nor swap it.
end note

== The attacker arms a login in its own tab, but will not finish it ==
A -> AP : open the attacker site
AP -> RP : POST /login/begin  (through the attacker's proxy)
activate RP
RP --> AP : { passkeyToken, challenge Ca }
AP -> AD : publish the challenge Ca in the dead-drop

== The victim's page is compromised and relays the sign-in ==
V -> VP : click "Sign in"
VP -> AD : fetch the pending challenge (cross-origin) -> Ca
VP -> AU : navigator.credentials.get(challenge = Ca, extensions: { dpop: true })
activate AU
V --> AU : user verification (biometric / PIN)
AU -> AU : generate a fresh ephemeral key pair SK\n(new for every login)
AU -> AU : write SK_pub into authenticatorData\n(dpop extension output, ED flag 0x80)
AU -> AU : sign authenticatorData || SHA256(clientDataJSON)\nwith the CREDENTIAL private key
AU -> AU : seal SK's private key inside the authenticator
AU --> VP : assertion over Ca, whose SIGNED authenticatorData carries SK_pub
deactivate AU
VP -> AD : hand the assertion to the dead-drop
note left of VP
  Nothing useful leaks: there is no page-held key, and the page cannot change
  SK_pub without invalidating the credential signature.
end note
VP -> V : "that did not work" (the victim never finished a login of their own)

== The attacker finishes the login, and is bound to a key it does not hold ==
AP -> AD : take the relayed assertion
AP -> RP : POST /login/finish  { assertion, passkeyToken }
RP -> RP : verify the credential signature over Ca;\nread SK_pub from the SIGNED authenticatorData;\nbind the session token to jkt = SHA256(SK_pub)
RP --> AP : { access_token, jkt }  (a real session for the victim, bound to SK)
deactivate RP

== The attacker tries to use the token, and is stuck ==
AP -> RP : GET /api/me  (Authorization: Passkey <access_token>)
RP --> AP : 401 - this session needs a fresh per-request proof
note right of AP
  A proof is an assertion signed by SK. SK's private key is sealed in the VICTIM's
  authenticator; this machine has no authenticator for it, and the key was never
  exposed to the relay. The attacker cannot produce the proof.
end note
AP -> RP : GET /api/me  (no valid proof)
RP --> AP : 401 - missing passkey proof
AP -> A : "Session captured, but locked"

note across #E7F6EC
  Both ways a separate binding key normally fails are closed:
    Exfiltration - SK's private key never leaves the authenticator, so the page cannot steal it.
    Substitution - SK_pub is named inside the credential-signed authenticatorData, so the page
                   cannot point the binding at a key the attacker holds.
  The relayed token is sender-constrained to a key only the victim's authenticator can wield,
  so it is inert anywhere else.
end note

@enduml
